        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1109">

                <pb facs="1006" n="692" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg692"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111/939</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé d’Affaires in Colombia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Harrison</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Bogotá</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1914-12-01T17:00:00-05:00">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">December 1, 1914, 5 p.m.</hi>
                    </date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received December 2, 3:15 p.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>Relative to Colombian neutrality. Newspapers publish telegram from Washington
                    reporting statement by officials of the Department to the effect that “The
                    United States will go so far as to permit European nations to send expeditions
                    to force compliance, if it is so deemed necessary.”</p>
                <p>Minister for Foreign Affairs asks if this is correct. He fears that it may
                    indicate intention to despatch such expeditions which would be entirely
                    undeserved.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harrison</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
